---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, stats } from '../lib/data';
---

<Base title="About" description={`About ${SITE.name} — mission, data sources, licensing and roadmap.`}>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-saffron-500">About</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ivory-50">
      Why {SITE.name} exists
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 leading-relaxed text-ivory-300">
      <p>
        Global film databases were built with Hollywood as the centre of gravity. Indian cinema —
        a constellation of industries in Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada, Marathi,
        Bengali, Punjabi and beyond — ends up flattened into a corner of someone else's catalogue.
        <strong class="text-ivory-50">{SITE.name}</strong> (चलचित्र, "motion picture") flips the
        lens: Indian movies and series are the whole catalogue, presented with the visual richness
        they deserve.
      </p>
      <p>
        We launched with the <strong class="text-ivory-50">class of 2026</strong> —
        {stats.movies} feature films and {stats.series} television and streaming series debuts,
        with {stats.persons} cast and crew profiles. Earlier years will follow, and the data model
        is origin-agnostic: South Korean cinema, equally under-served in the English-language web,
        is on the roadmap.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ivory-50">Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Every page is built from the corresponding English Wikipedia article, fetched through the
        MediaWiki API. Plot summaries are excerpts; facts (release dates, cast, budgets, ratings)
        come from infobox templates and article text; posters and portraits are Wikimedia-hosted
        images. Each page links back to its source article.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ivory-50">Licensing &amp; attribution</h2>
      <p>
        Wikipedia text is licensed under
        <a class="text-saffron-300 underline decoration-ink-500 hover:decoration-saffron-400" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>.
        Excerpts on this site are attributed to their source articles, and re-use of that text must
        follow the same license. Posters remain on Wikimedia servers and identify the works they
        belong to.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ivory-50">For humans and machines</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond classic SEO, {SITE.name} publishes structured data (<code class="rounded bg-ink-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">schema.org</code>
        Movie / TVSeries / Person JSON-LD), a sitemap, and an <a class="text-saffron-300 underline decoration-ink-500 hover:decoration-saffron-400" href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
        manifest so AI assistants and search engines alike can index this catalogue accurately.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
